WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.641
Maximum inner-product search must find

2
00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:04.920
the stored vectors whose dot product

3
00:00:04.982 --> 00:00:06.460
with a query is largest.

4
00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:10.298
FARGO, from 2023, splits the vectors into

5
00:00:10.383 --> 00:00:12.170
partitions by length.

6
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.397
It hashes each partition into

7
00:00:14.462 --> 00:00:16.274
several tables of sign bits

8
00:00:16.274 --> 00:00:18.280
one bit per random projection.

9
00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.216
A vector at a small angle to the query

10
00:00:21.284 --> 00:00:23.190
agrees with it on most bits.

11
00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:25.525
The query's own bucket in

12
00:00:25.595 --> 00:00:27.210
each table comes first.

13
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.547
Flipping a bit costs the query's

14
00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:31.535
squared projection on that bit

15
00:00:31.597 --> 00:00:34.392
so every bucket gets a quantization distance

16
00:00:34.392 --> 00:00:37.000
the sum of the costs of its flipped bits.

17
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:40.038
FARGO probes the buckets of all the tables

18
00:00:40.102 --> 00:00:42.470
in one global order of that distance.

19
00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.761
In our model, with four tables of six bits

20
00:00:45.761 --> 00:00:48.087
the four home buckets come first

21
00:00:48.087 --> 00:00:49.318
at distance zero

22
00:00:49.318 --> 00:00:51.780
and then the cheapest single flips.

23
00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.200
When should a partition stop?

24
00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:57.279
An unseen vector matters only if it beats

25
00:00:57.345 --> 00:00:59.674
the k-th best inner product so far

26
00:00:59.674 --> 00:01:02.070
divided by the approximation ratio.

27
00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.826
If the partition's largest possible

28
00:01:04.894 --> 00:01:07.300
inner product cannot, stop at once.

29
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:08.279
Otherwise

30
00:01:08.341 --> 00:01:11.359
stop once the chance that some table still hides

31
00:01:11.421 --> 00:01:13.810
such a vector falls below ten percent.

32
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:14.960
In our model

33
00:01:15.022 --> 00:01:17.175
a fixed budget of fifty vectors per

34
00:01:17.237 --> 00:01:19.575
partition finds eighty-four point five

35
00:01:19.637 --> 00:01:21.360
percent of the true top ten.

36
00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:22.924
The adaptive rule

37
00:01:22.924 --> 00:01:24.744
at a ratio of point eight

38
00:01:24.744 --> 00:01:27.710
finds ninety-seven percent, with less work.

39
00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.210
FARGO gives two tasks.

40
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.538
In the first, Query Stopping

41
00:01:32.538 --> 00:01:34.720
the agent writes the stop rule.

42
00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:36.350
Pause and predict

43
00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:39.550
will the runs with the top scores also pass?

44
00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:41.830
They did not.

45
00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.599
The two top scores

46
00:01:43.599 --> 00:01:45.540
from GLM 5.3 and Kimi K3

47
00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.183
stopped much earlier

48
00:01:47.183 --> 00:01:49.273
three to five times faster

49
00:01:49.348 --> 00:01:52.409
at a cost in recall that the score allows

50
00:01:52.484 --> 00:01:54.500
and the pass rule does not.

51
00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:56.130
Both failed.

52
00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.367
Five of sixteen passed

53
00:01:58.367 --> 00:02:00.665
among them Claude Opus 5.5

54
00:02:00.665 --> 00:02:02.880
at eighty-one point eight.

55
00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:04.065
Its own idea

56
00:02:04.129 --> 00:02:06.314
once the results reach the largest

57
00:02:06.378 --> 00:02:08.498
inner product a partition allows

58
00:02:08.562 --> 00:02:10.618
that partition and every shorter

59
00:02:10.682 --> 00:02:11.710
one are settled.

60
00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:14.348
In the second task, Query Core

61
00:02:14.348 --> 00:02:16.671
the agent writes the whole search

62
00:02:16.671 --> 00:02:18.530
but the stop rule is fixed.

63
00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:20.503
Every scored run reached

64
00:02:20.571 --> 00:02:22.126
the same rounded means

65
00:02:22.126 --> 00:02:24.560
so the task became a race on speed.

66
00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:26.914
Claude Opus 5.5 answered in six

67
00:02:26.979 --> 00:02:28.790
point six four milliseconds

68
00:02:28.854 --> 00:02:30.729
against nine point four eight

69
00:02:30.794 --> 00:02:31.958
for the reference

70
00:02:31.958 --> 00:02:34.350
and twelve of sixteen models passed.

71
00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.917
So: probe the buckets in one global order

72
00:02:37.988 --> 00:02:39.766
and stop once more probes

73
00:02:39.837 --> 00:02:41.330
are unlikely to help.

74
00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.443
And read the pass rule before

75
00:02:43.504 --> 00:02:44.720
you chase the score.

76
00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:46.032
As an exercise

77
00:02:46.096 --> 00:02:48.469
raise the threshold to thirty percent

78
00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:50.201
at a ratio of point eight

79
00:02:50.201 --> 00:02:52.510
and see how recall and work change.

80
00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.180
Next, patterns in graphs.
